[{"id": "R013", "text": "The speaker tests the edge and moves a nail without changing the failure, then states where the nail was placed and moves a seed instead. They continue the explanation while leaving the nail in place and noting that the edge remains cold."}, {"id": "R014", "text": "The speaker places the two instruments side by side, marks their difference, and prioritizes the explanation over a label. They keep the instrument with the small change and write the difference on the wall."}, {"id": "R015", "text": "The speaker taps the edge once, repeats an explanation they judge incorrect, and keeps both the correct and incorrect versions visible while setting the incorrect one aside. They write the correct version on a scrap and keep it."}, {"id": "R016", "text": "The speaker checks a small change and proposes marking the failure edge and testing one explanation before changing anything. If it fails again, they would leave the old explanation alone and keep the new one on the bench, while acknowledging uncertainty about which is correct."}, {"id": "R017", "text": "The speaker photographs a discrepancy and checks a second explanation. After accidentally pressing a second button, they move it away, try a third option that works, and then return to check the second model before repeating the mistake."}, {"id": "R018", "text": "The speaker sets the old explanation aside, compares the instruments, and tests whether the new behavior changes the result. They keep the old part visible and mark the new edge if it does; otherwise, they leave the old part unchanged and state that the new tool relocated the failure rather than disproving the old explanation."}, {"id": "R019", "text": "The speaker keeps the old watch beside the broken one, counts wear on the handle, taps the old watch twice, and leaves the new watch beside it. They remain close to the old watch while keeping their thumb tucked under its handle."}, {"id": "R020", "text": "The speaker joins two pieces, identifies the failure location, and holds the smaller piece against the wall. After comparing the explanation with the failure, they set one piece aside and distinguish the failure from the explanation."}, {"id": "R021", "text": "The speaker checks both sides of the board and leaves the failure visible. If they find the wrong note, they leave the explanation with the test board; if the note remains after checking both sides, they keep the explanation."}, {"id": "R022", "text": "The speaker moves a piece, observes the watch tick, and checks which piece is present before putting the missing one back. They write a new account, check the edges, observe another tick, and keep the old piece while leaving the two pieces separate."}, {"id": "R023", "text": "The speaker retrieves a seed tin, holds it against their chest, compares it with an older tin, and notes a different edge and origin. They move the tin closer to a stranger's face, examine the failure, and identify the other tin as theirs."}, {"id": "R024", "text": "The speaker rubs the gears together, finds a tooth in their palm, and notes that the result is the same despite a difference. They select the explanation that matters most, leave a loose bolt beside it, and conclude that the result is unchanged but the explanation is wrong."}]
